{"name":"Candied Apple","hex":"#b95b6d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"candied","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of candy"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["coated or encrusted with sugar","preserved in sugar or syrup by baking till it becomes translucent"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/candied"},{"term":"apple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, firm, round fruit produced by a tree of the genus Malus.","The fruit of the tree Malus domestica, chiefly with a green, red, or yellow skin, cultivated in temperate climates for cidermaking, cooking, and eating. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make (something) appear like an apple (noun sense 1.1).","To become like an apple.\n\n To collect fir-cones.\n Of a flower bud or vegetable (especially a root vegetable): to grow into the shape of an apple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Candy apple","blocks":[{"text":"Candy apples or toffee apples are whole apples covered in a sugar candy coating, with a stick inserted as a handle. These are a common treat at fall festivals in Western culture in the Northern Hemisphere, such as Halloween and Guy Fawkes Night, because these festivals occur in the wake of annual apple harvests. Although candy apples and caramel apples may seem similar, they are made using distinctly different processes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Tastee-Candy-Apple-Red-Caramel-wPeanuts.jpg/330px-Tastee-Candy-Apple-Red-Caramel-wPeanuts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candy_apple","wikidata":"Q1358074"},{"title":"Candied fruit","blocks":[{"text":"Candied fruit, also known as glacé fruit, is whole fruit, smaller pieces of fruit, or pieces of peel, placed in heated sugar syrup, which absorbs the moisture from within the fruit and eventually preserves it. Depending on the size and type of fruit, this process can take from several days to several months. This process of preservation, which has been used since the 14th century, allows the fruit to remain edible for up to a year."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Candied_Fruit_-_La_Boquer%C3%ADa.jpg/330px-Candied_Fruit_-_La_Boquer%C3%ADa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candied_fruit","wikidata":"Q1163138"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple","wikidata":"Q89"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Pommes_d_amour.jpg/500px-Pommes_d_amour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Pommes_d_amour.jpg/1280px-Pommes_d_amour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Pommes_d_amour.jpg/500px-Pommes_d_amour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pommes d'amour on display","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APommes_d_amour.jpg"},